            {
              "id": "e3f3f5b3-a65e-3f9d-8608-08651b8746cb",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk due to the destination buffer's capacity being unknown and the count argument not being proven safe. The code copies a string literal into a buffer using a maximum size macro minus one, but there is no evidence that the buffer is large enough to accommodate the data. The destination is a pointer type, and there is no information about its actual size or allocation. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings if the source string length matches or exceeds the count. The source is a string literal, which slightly increases the chance of safety, but the lack of buffer size validation and null-termination makes this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow and ensure proper null-termination, explicitly set the last byte of the destination buffer to the null character after the copy. This ensures the string is always null-terminated, even if the source string is truncated.\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Unknown event received\", MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis approach assumes that `MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE` accurately reflects the size of the destination buffer. If the actual buffer size is not guaranteed, validate the buffer size before copying to prevent overflows.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, consider using `snprintf` to safely copy and null-terminate the string, as it always null-terminates the output (provided the size is greater than zero):\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE, \"%s\", \"Unknown event received\");\n```\n\nThis method is safer and more robust, as it prevents buffer overflows and guarantees null-termination.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-120",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html"
                  },
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-119",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html"
                  },
