            {
              "id": "af50df7a-f08a-31a7-aca5-e0ef24fb0dc2",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null source pointer, and the automated verdict also marks it as genuine. The code copies a string from a potentially null source pointer to a destination buffer using a fixed maximum size minus one. There are no explicit checks to ensure the source pointer is not null before the copy operation, which could lead to a crash or undefined behavior if the source is null. The destination is an address-of expression, so it is guaranteed to be non-null. The source is a C-style string, which slightly increases the chance of a false positive, but the lack of null checks for the source is a significant risk. No buffer overlap is possible, and the destination buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, but the main issue is the missing null check for the source pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. Add an explicit check for the source pointer before the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // Or handle error as appropriate\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is only written to if the source is valid, and also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to handle the case where the source pointer is null by setting the destination buffer to an empty string, you can do the following:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior and ensures the destination buffer is always a valid string.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
