            {
              "id": "9c2b6f9a-cda7-365c-81b8-289edb7b7ad8",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the source pointer may be null, and there are no explicit checks to ensure the source is valid before copying. The risk is further highlighted by the classification indicating undefined behavior due to possible null pointers. The destination is a non-null address-of expression, so the main concern is the lack of validation for the source. The use of a constant for the copy size does not mitigate the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. The probability of this being a false positive is low because the analysis found no evidence of a guard or other mitigating control.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the result of `final_message.str().c_str()` before calling the copy function. For example:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    // Optionally, ensure null-termination\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle the error, e.g., set message to empty string or log an error\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy operation is only performed when the source is valid, preventing undefined behavior from null pointer dereference.\n\n___\n"
