            {
              "id": "b0ab0caf-6aca-3350-9b0e-e4f7c19a0af5",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being passed as the source to the string copy operation. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in this assessment. The source expression is derived from a C-style string, which increases the risk if not properly checked for null. There are no explicit checks to ensure the source is not null before the copy operation, and the destination is confirmed to be non-null. The absence of a null-termination step after the copy further increases the risk of undefined behavior if the source is null or if the copy does not include a null terminator. These factors collectively indicate a real risk of a null pointer dereference or buffer misuse.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check for the source string before calling the copy function. If the source is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., set the destination to an empty string or return an error code).\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is not written with a null source, and also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to ensure the destination buffer is always null-terminated and avoid undefined behavior, you can use a safer string copy function such as `strlcpy` (if available), or manually ensure null-termination after the copy. Always check the source for null before copying.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
              },
