            {
              "id": "bda1566d-65d9-3bec-8019-8f32a4e525ec",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code uses a string copy operation where the source pointer may be null, and there are no explicit checks to ensure the source is valid before copying. The classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior if the source pointer is null, which can lead to program crashes or other unpredictable behavior. The destination is an address-of expression, so it is guaranteed to be non-null, but the source is derived from a string object's c_str() method, which could potentially return a null pointer if the string is empty or uninitialized. No guard is present to check for null pointers before the copy, and there is no explicit null-termination after the copy. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy, ensure that the source pointer returned by the string object's c_str() method is not null. If it is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., skip the copy, log an error, or set the destination to an empty string). Also, ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy to prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // Set as empty string if source is null\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to ensure robust handling and avoid undefined behavior, you can use safer string copy functions such as `strlcpy` (if available) or explicitly check the source and always null-terminate the destination buffer.\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    size_t n = MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE;\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, n - 1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[n - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
