              "id": "11c296bd-c032-30e2-aa04-1da40e326817",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null pointer being passed as the source argument. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', which further confirms the risk. The source of the copy operation is a C-style string, which increases the likelihood of null pointer issues if not properly checked. There are no explicit checks to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the copy operation, and the destination is a pointer type without a known fixed capacity. The absence of a null-termination assignment after the copy and the lack of a guard condition to verify the validity of the source pointer both contribute to the risk. Therefore, this is a genuine issue that could lead to undefined behavior if the source pointer is null at runtime.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that the source pointer is not null. Add an explicit check to verify that `final_message.str().c_str()` does not return a null pointer. If it is null, handle the error appropriately (e.g., by setting the destination to an empty string or logging an error).\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // or handle error as needed\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source pointer is valid, and also guarantees null-termination of the destination buffer.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you are certain that `final_message.str().c_str()` should never be null (for example, if `final_message` is always a valid stringstream), you can add an assertion to catch programming errors during development:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nassert(src_ptr != nullptr);\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis will help detect unexpected null pointers during testing.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\nTo further improve safety, always ensure the destination buffer is null-terminated after using `strncpy`, as it does not guarantee null-termination if the source string is longer than the specified count:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), final_message.str().c_str(), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis prevents potential string over-read or non-null-terminated buffer issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n\n___\n"
