            {
              "id": "79f269c2-0568-3140-8ae4-fb91145c9f2d",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility of a null source pointer, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The source of the copy operation is a C-style string, which may be null if the string object is empty or uninitialized, and there are no explicit checks to ensure it is not null before calling the function. The destination is a pointer to a structure member, which is non-null, but the lack of a null check on the source pointer can lead to undefined behavior if a null pointer is passed. No buffer overlap is possible, but the absence of a null check on the source is a significant risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that the source pointer is not null to prevent undefined behavior. Add an explicit check for the source pointer before calling the copy function:\n\n```cpp\nconst char* src_ptr = final_message.str().c_str();\nif (src_ptr != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), src_ptr, MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n} else {\n    reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[0] = '\\0'; // Set as empty string if source is null\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is only written to if the source is valid, and always remains null-terminated.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to guarantee that the destination buffer is always a valid string, you can use a safer string copy function that handles null pointers gracefully, or wrap the logic in a helper function:\n\n```cpp\nvoid safe_strncpy(char* dst, const char* src, size_t n) {\n    if (src) {\n        strncpy(dst, src, n);\n        dst[n] = '\\0';\n    } else {\n        dst[0] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage:\nsafe_strncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), final_message.str().c_str(), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1);\n```\n\nThis approach centralizes the null check and ensures consistent handling throughout the codebase.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n\n___\n"
