            {
              "id": "75a8d450-82c5-3f82-b7b9-bc7a902655df",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk due to the destination buffer's capacity being unknown and the count argument not being proven safe. The code copies a string literal into a buffer using a maximum size macro minus one, but there is no evidence that the buffer is large enough to accommodate the data. The destination is a pointer type, and there is no information about its actual size or allocation. Additionally, there is no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to unterminated strings. The source is a string literal, which is generally safe, but this does not mitigate the risk of buffer overflow if the destination is too small. There are no guards or checks to ensure the destination buffer is large enough, and the count is not derived from the buffer's actual capacity. The analysis did not detect any risk of overlapping memory regions or null pointers at this call site.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo mitigate the risk of buffer overflow, ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied string and the null terminator. After copying, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings. For example, immediately after the copy, add:\n\n```cpp\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nAdditionally, verify that `MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE` does not exceed the actual size of the `message` field in `data_item`. If possible, use `sizeof(data_item->message)` instead of a macro to ensure correctness.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf the actual size of the destination buffer is known, use it directly in the copy operation to avoid mismatches. For example, if `data_item->message` is an array of known size, use:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), \"Event type None received\", sizeof(data_item->message) - 1);\nreinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message)[sizeof(data_item->message) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures the copy does not exceed the buffer's capacity and that the string is always null-terminated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 3\nConsider using safer string copy functions, such as `snprintf`, which always null-terminate the destination buffer:\n\n```cpp\nsnprintf(reinterpret_cast<char *>(&data_item->message), MAX_EVENT_NOTIFICATION_MSG_SIZE, \"Event type None received\");\n```\n\nThis approach reduces the risk of buffer overflows and unterminated strings.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Glossary: Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
