            {
              "id": "e6c1ef5d-5a8d-36c8-ae99-94952fcaca2a",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no explicit checks to prevent this. The verdict for this call site is also marked as 'GENUINE', confirming the high confidence in the issue. The source is a C-style string, which increases the risk if not properly checked. Both the source and destination pointers may be null, as there are no guards in place to ensure their validity before the buffer operation. The use of a length expression ('len-1') without a clear relationship to the actual buffer size further increases the risk. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can lead to string handling errors. All these factors contribute to the assessment that this is a real and actionable security or correctness issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the copy operation, ensure that both the destination and source pointers are not null, and that the length is appropriate for the destination buffer. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after copying to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && vendor_name.c_str() != nullptr && len > 0) {\n    strncpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), len - 1);\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior due to null pointers and ensures the destination string is always null-terminated, reducing the risk of buffer overflows and string handling errors.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, consider using a safer string copy function that handles null-termination and checks for null pointers, such as `strlcpy` (if available), or implement similar logic manually:\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && vendor_name.c_str() != nullptr && len > 0) {\n    size_t src_len = strlen(vendor_name.c_str());\n    size_t copy_len = (src_len < (size_t)(len - 1)) ? src_len : (size_t)(len - 1);\n    memcpy(name, vendor_name.c_str(), copy_len);\n    name[copy_len] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that only the appropriate number of bytes are copied and the destination is always null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
