            {
              "id": "d92a9f20-1715-3246-9d9a-e502e22a5f68",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string from a C++ string's c_str() method into a destination buffer using strncpy, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that either the source (description.c_str()) or the destination (policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description) is non-null before the operation. If either pointer is null, this will result in undefined behavior, which can lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities. The risk is further increased because the destination type is a pointer and there is no evidence that the buffer size is being derived from the actual capacity of the destination. The absence of a guard or explicit null checks, combined with the use of a count expression that is not directly tied to the destination's size, further supports the assessment that this is a genuine issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the string copy operation, add explicit checks to ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if either pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (policy && description.c_str() && policy->policies && policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description) {\n    strncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, description.c_str(), RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\n    policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy only occurs when all pointers are valid, and it also explicitly null-terminates the destination buffer to prevent potential string overflows.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to handle the case where the source or destination is null gracefully, you can log an error or return an error code before attempting the copy operation:\n\n```cpp\nif (!policy || !description.c_str() || !policy->policies || !policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description) {\n    // Handle error: log or return\n    // fprintf(stderr, \"Null pointer encountered in policy description copy\\n\");\n    return ERROR_CODE;\n}\nstrncpy(policy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description, description.c_str(), RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1);\npolicy->policies[policy->num_supported].policy_description[RSMI_MAX_POLICY_NAME-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis ensures that the function fails safely if a null pointer is detected.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
