            {
              "id": "51972bad-11a3-33a2-a9c7-7b7177282a69",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string from a source pointer to a destination buffer using a function that does not perform any checks to ensure that either pointer is non-null. The source is a C-style string, and both the source and destination pointers may be null at this point in the code, as there are no explicit checks before the copy operation. This can lead to undefined behavior, such as a crash or potential security issue, if either pointer is null. The risk is further increased because the destination type is a pointer to a structure, and the buffer size is not explicitly derived from the destination's capacity. No explicit null-termination is enforced after the copy, which may also result in a non-null-terminated string if the source is too long. These factors indicate a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that both the destination and source pointers are not null. Also, explicitly null-terminate the destination buffer after the copy to prevent unterminated strings. For example:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info && !model_name.empty()) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by checking for null pointers and ensures the destination buffer is always null-terminated, even if the source string is too long.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if you want to be extra defensive, you can add explicit checks for both the destination structure and the source string pointer, and handle the case where either is null:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_info != nullptr && model_name.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(cpu_info->model_name, model_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    cpu_info->model_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: one or both pointers are null\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs when both pointers are valid, and provides a place to handle error conditions if they are not.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n\n___\n"
              },
