              "id": "37cdc37c-048b-35e7-9d93-b1aa2ebfb859",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code performs a memory copy operation without verifying that either the source or destination pointers are non-null. The analysis indicates that both the source (bitmask.data()) and destination (cpu_set) pointers may be null at this point in the code, and there are no explicit checks to prevent undefined behavior. The risk is further confirmed by the classification and the verdict, which both indicate a high-confidence issue. The absence of overlap between source and destination reduces the risk of memory corruption, but the lack of null pointer checks can still lead to crashes or other undefined behavior if either pointer is null.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null, and that the size to copy is greater than zero. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_set != nullptr && bitmask.data() != nullptr && cpu_set_size > 0) {\n    std::memcpy(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n}\n```\n\nThis check ensures that the memory copy only occurs when both pointers are valid and the size is meaningful. If either pointer is null, the operation is safely skipped or handled as appropriate for your application logic.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if skipping the operation is not acceptable, you can handle the error explicitly by returning an error code or throwing an exception when a null pointer is detected:\n\n```cpp\nif (cpu_set == nullptr || bitmask.data() == nullptr) {\n    // Handle error: log, throw, or return error code\n    throw std::invalid_argument(\"Null pointer passed to memcpy\");\n}\nstd::memcpy(cpu_set, bitmask.data(), cpu_set_size * sizeof(uint64_t));\n```\n\nThis approach makes the error explicit and easier to debug.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
