            {
              "id": "05f0bc09-00f8-3a93-a519-2fba50f2386c",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string into a destination buffer using a function that does not perform any checks to ensure that the source or destination pointers are not null. The analysis indicates that both the source (driver_name.c_str()) and the destination (info->driver_name) may be null at this point in the code, and there are no explicit checks to prevent this. If either pointer is null, this will result in undefined behavior, which can lead to crashes or security vulnerabilities. The use of a constant for the copy length does not mitigate the risk of null pointer dereference. The risk is further increased because the source is a C-style string, and the destination is a pointer type with no known capacity or null check. No explicit null-termination is enforced after the copy, which could also lead to string handling issues.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, add explicit checks to ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (info != nullptr && info->driver_name != nullptr && !driver_name.empty()) {\n    strncpy(info->driver_name, driver_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\n    info->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy only occurs if both pointers are valid and the source string is not empty. Additionally, it enforces null-termination of the destination buffer to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if the structure of `amdsmi_driver_info_t` is known and you can guarantee that `driver_name` is always valid, you can use assertions to catch programming errors during development:\n\n```cpp\nassert(info != nullptr);\nassert(info->driver_name != nullptr);\nassert(!driver_name.empty());\nstrncpy(info->driver_name, driver_name.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\ninfo->driver_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis will terminate the program in debug builds if a null pointer is encountered, helping to catch issues early.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
