            {
              "id": "c6f95e31-9f3a-3ed1-ab02-e1fb546e855e",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the code copies a string from a potentially null source pointer to a potentially null destination pointer without any explicit checks for null values. The risk is that if either the source or destination pointer is null, the program may exhibit undefined behavior, such as crashing or corrupting memory. The destination buffer is a pointer type, and there is no evidence that its capacity is checked or that the length argument is derived from the buffer's actual size. Additionally, there are no explicit null-termination steps or guards to prevent issues. The source is a C-style string, which increases the risk if not properly validated. The SAST tool has flagged this as a high-confidence issue, and the verdict is 'GENUINE', indicating that this is not a false positive and should be addressed.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, add explicit checks to ensure that both the destination and source pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior if either pointer is invalid.\n\n```cpp\nif (info != nullptr && info->driver_date != nullptr && !driver_date.empty()) {\n    strncpy(info->driver_date, driver_date.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1);\n    info->driver_date[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy only occurs when both pointers are valid and the source string is not empty. Additionally, explicitly null-terminating the destination buffer after the copy helps prevent string-related vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, you can use safer string copy functions that handle null pointers and buffer sizes more robustly, such as `strlcpy` (if available), or wrap the copy in a utility function that performs all necessary checks.\n\n```cpp\nvoid safe_strncpy(char *dst, const char *src, size_t size) {\n    if (dst != nullptr && src != nullptr && size > 0) {\n        strncpy(dst, src, size - 1);\n        dst[size - 1] = '\\0';\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage:\nsafe_strncpy(info->driver_date, driver_date.c_str(), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n```\n\nThis encapsulates the safety checks and ensures consistent handling throughout the codebase.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
