            {
              "id": "a6cfabca-f3ca-3ce8-8270-e6f1abbdd75f",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as 'UB Risk: possible src/dst overlap (undefined behavior)' and the verdict is 'GENUINE'. The code copies data from a source to a destination using a function that is not safe if the source and destination memory regions overlap. Dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior. Additionally, the destination pointer may be null, and there is no explicit check to prevent this. There is no evidence of a guard to ensure the destination is valid, and the function does not guarantee null-termination of the destination buffer. These factors increase the risk of undefined behavior and potential security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd explicit checks to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. Also, verify that the destination pointer is not null before use.\n\n```cpp\nif (info != nullptr && (reinterpret_cast<void*>(info->name) != reinterpret_cast<void*>(vbios.name))) {\n    strncpy(info->name, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring the source and destination do not overlap and the destination is valid.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and cannot be ruled out, use a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, and ensure the destination is not null.\n\n```cpp\nif (info != nullptr) {\n    memmove(info->name, reinterpret_cast<char *>(vbios.name), AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach uses `memmove`, which is safe for overlapping memory regions, and adds a null-termination step to prevent string-related issues.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
