            {
              "id": "4b49ee2d-b316-3ba0-a06c-01e2ece8e318",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the destination pointer used in the buffer copy operation may be null, and there are no explicit checks to ensure it is valid before use. The operation copies a string literal into a structure member using a constant maximum length, which is generally safe for buffer overflows, but the absence of a null pointer check on the destination introduces a risk of undefined behavior if the structure pointer is null. The source is a string literal, so it cannot be null, but the destination pointer is not guaranteed to be valid. This is a common source of crashes or security issues in C/C++ code.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore copying the string into the destination buffer, ensure that the destination pointer and the buffer member are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```c\nif (info != NULL && info->vram_vendor != NULL) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, \"UNKNOWN\", AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n}\n```\n\nIf `vram_vendor` is a fixed-size array within the structure and cannot be null, only check that `info` is not null:\n\n```c\nif (info != NULL) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, \"UNKNOWN\", AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
