            {
              "id": "aff4f22c-8635-3534-9d43-33bdc7de302c",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because it is marked as 'UB Risk: possible src/dst overlap (undefined behavior)' and the verdict is 'GENUINE'. The code copies data from a source to a destination buffer using a function that is not safe if the source and destination memory regions overlap. Dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may overlap, which can result in undefined behavior. Additionally, there is no explicit check to ensure that the destination pointer is not null, which could also lead to a crash. The absence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy further increases the risk of string-related bugs. These factors make this a high-confidence, actionable issue that should be remediated.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nAdd a check to ensure that the source and destination memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, handle the copy safely or avoid it entirely. For example:\n\n```c\nif (info != NULL && (info->vram_vendor != brand)) {\n    strncpy(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis ensures that the copy only occurs if the source and destination do not overlap, and also adds a null-terminator to prevent unterminated strings.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible and must be handled, use a function that is safe for overlapping regions, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. For example:\n\n```c\nif (info != NULL) {\n    memmove(info->vram_vendor, brand, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH);\n    info->vram_vendor[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach safely handles overlapping memory and ensures the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n___\n"
              },
