            {
              "id": "8b953c96-84da-32f3-a412-7b2b8870a2c8",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the buffer copy operation. The code uses a buffer copy function where the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory, which is not allowed and can lead to unpredictable results or program crashes. The destination buffer is a pointer with no known capacity, and there is no evidence of a guard to ensure the destination is valid or non-null. Additionally, the analysis determined through data flow that the source and destination may alias each other. There is also no explicit null-termination after the copy, which can further increase the risk of security issues. The verdict is marked as genuine due to the high confidence in the undefined behavior risk.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nTo prevent undefined behavior when copying memory where the source and destination may overlap, add a check to ensure that the memory regions do not overlap before performing the copy. If overlap is possible, use a temporary buffer or adjust the logic to avoid overlap.\n\n```c\nif (name != proc_id && !regions_overlap(name, proc_id, len)) {\n    strncpy(name, proc_id, len);\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap: use a temporary buffer or memmove if appropriate\n}\n\n// Helper function to check for overlap\nint regions_overlap(const void *a, const void *b, size_t len) {\n    const char *pa = (const char *)a;\n    const char *pb = (const char *)b;\n    return (pa < pb + len) && (pb < pa + len);\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nAlternatively, if overlapping memory regions are possible and unavoidable, use the `memmove` function instead of `strncpy`, as `memmove` is designed to handle overlapping memory safely. Note that `memmove` does not null-terminate strings, so you may need to add a null terminator manually if working with strings.\n\n```c\nmemmove(name, proc_id, len);\n// Optionally, ensure null-termination if copying strings\nif (len > 0) {\n    name[len - 1] = '\\0';\n}\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
