            {
              "id": "1e8a628d-b4db-3245-9bb3-f031f781c813",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a high risk: the destination buffer's capacity is not known, and the number of bytes being copied is not proven to be within safe bounds. The destination is a pointer to a character array, but there is no information about its size, and the length argument is taken directly from a structure member without any visible bounds checking. There are no explicit guards or null-termination after the copy, and neither the source nor destination is likely to be null. The analysis did not detect any overlap between source and destination, which slightly reduces the risk, but the lack of capacity checks and direct use of a potentially untrusted length value make this a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the number of bytes to be copied does not exceed the available space in the destination buffer. If the size of the destination buffer is known, compare it with the length value and only copy up to the minimum of the two. For example:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t dst_capacity = /* actual size of cper_data from data_idx onward */;\nsize_t copy_len = header->record_length;\nif (copy_len > dst_capacity) {\n    // Handle error: insufficient space\n    copy_len = dst_capacity;\n}\nstd::memcpy(&cper_data[data_idx], reinterpret_cast<const char*>(header), copy_len);\n```\n\nReplace the comment with actual logic to determine the available space in `cper_data` starting at `data_idx`. Always validate external or structure-provided lengths before using them in memory operations.\n\n___\n"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [],
                "severity": "LOW",
                "kind": "FAIL"
              }
