            {
              "id": "31bd3edb-d331-3f1d-a44d-ef649b2d147e",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is classified as a genuine vulnerability because the analysis identified a risk of undefined behavior due to possible overlap between the source and destination memory regions in the buffer copy operation. The code uses a standard string copy function to copy data from one buffer to another, but dataflow analysis indicates that the source and destination may refer to overlapping memory regions. This can result in unpredictable program behavior, data corruption, or crashes. Additionally, the destination pointer may be null, which could also lead to a crash if not properly checked. There is no explicit guard to ensure the destination is valid, and the function does not guarantee that the source and destination are distinct. The presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy does not mitigate the undefined behavior risk from overlapping buffers. These factors confirm the issue as a genuine vulnerability that requires remediation.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore performing the buffer copy, ensure that the source and destination buffers do not overlap. Add a runtime check to verify that the memory regions are distinct. If overlap is possible, avoid using functions like `strncpy` that do not support overlapping regions.\n\n```cpp\nif (market_name != name && !(market_name > name && market_name < name + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1) && !(name > market_name && name < market_name + AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1)) {\n    std::strncpy(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\n    market_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle overlap case appropriately, e.g., use a temporary buffer or memmove\n}\n```\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf overlapping memory regions are possible, use a function that safely handles overlap, such as `memmove`, instead of `strncpy`. This ensures defined behavior even if the source and destination overlap.\n\n```cpp\nstd::memmove(market_name, name, AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1);\nmarket_name[AMDSMI_MAX_STRING_LENGTH - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [NIST Buffer Overflow](https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/buffer_overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n\n___\n"
