            {
              "id": "17a0f40a-2e6f-3df8-89a9-ef7793a9a277",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a C-style string into a destination buffer using a length argument, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that either pointer is valid before the operation. If either pointer is null, this can lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior. The risk is further increased because the destination is a pointer with no known capacity, and the length argument is not derived from the buffer's actual size. There are no explicit null-termination steps after the copy, and no guard conditions are present to prevent underflow or null pointer dereference. These factors all contribute to the assessment that this is a genuine and actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, ensure that both the source and destination pointers are not null. Add explicit checks to prevent undefined behavior if either pointer is null. Also, consider verifying that the length argument is valid and that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the copied data.\n\n```cpp\nif (version != nullptr && empty.c_str() != nullptr && len > 0) {\n    std::strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), len-1);\n    version[len-1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n}\n```\n\nThis approach prevents the function from attempting to copy if either pointer is null and ensures the destination buffer is null-terminated after the copy.\n\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you cannot guarantee that the pointers are always valid, consider using safer string handling functions or wrapping the operation in a function that performs all necessary checks. For example:\n\n```cpp\nvoid safe_strncpy(char* dst, const char* src, size_t len) {\n    if (dst == nullptr || src == nullptr || len == 0) return;\n    std::strncpy(dst, src, len-1);\n    dst[len-1] = '\\0';\n}\n// Usage:\nsafe_strncpy(version, empty.c_str(), len);\n```\n\nThis encapsulates the safety checks and ensures consistent handling throughout your codebase.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n\n\n___\n"
              },
