              "id": "d978da02-dbc8-38d8-8dd5-346f056d7e6a",
              "name": "Problematic C function detected (memcpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to possible null pointer dereference, and the verdict is marked as genuine. The source of the copy operation is a C-style string obtained from a string object, but there is no explicit check to ensure that the source pointer is not null before the memory copy occurs. The destination is a character array, which is less likely to be null, but the source may be null if the string object is empty or uninitialized. The absence of a guard condition to check for null pointers increases the risk of undefined behavior. Additionally, the presence of an explicit null-terminator assignment after the copy does not mitigate the risk of dereferencing a null pointer. Therefore, this issue should be addressed to prevent potential crashes or security vulnerabilities.\n\n## In Context Remediation\nBefore performing the memory copy, ensure that the source string object is not null and that its c_str() method does not return a null pointer. Add an explicit check to verify that the source pointer is valid:\n\n```cpp\nif (!newString.empty()) {\n    std::memcpy(buffer, newString.c_str(), copy_len);\n    buffer[copy_len] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle empty string case appropriately\n    buffer[0] = '\\0';\n}\n```\nThis approach prevents undefined behavior by ensuring that the source pointer is never null when passed to memcpy. Adjust the handling of the empty string case as needed for your application logic.\n\n___\n"
