            {
              "id": "2a44b698-f2c8-347c-8d06-3d625583e114",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null, and there are no explicit checks to prevent this. The code copies data from a C-style string to a character buffer using a function that does not inherently protect against null pointers. The source is a C-style string, which slightly increases the chance of a false positive, but both the source and destination pointers may be null at this point in the code, and there are no guards in place to prevent this. Additionally, the buffer's capacity is not explicitly known, and the count argument is not directly derived from the buffer's size, which further increases the risk. The presence of a null-terminator assignment after the copy is a minor positive, but it does not mitigate the risk of dereferencing a null pointer.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying data from the source string to the destination buffer, ensure that both pointers are not null. Add explicit checks for both the source and destination pointers to prevent undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (buff != nullptr && contents.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff-1] = '\\0';\n} else {\n    // Handle error: one or both pointers are null\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy operation is only performed when both pointers are valid, preventing undefined behavior and potential crashes.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you want to provide a fallback or default value when either pointer is null, you can initialize the buffer to an empty string or a safe default value:\n\n```cpp\nif (buff != nullptr && contents.c_str() != nullptr) {\n    std::strncpy(buff, contents.c_str(), sizeOfBuff-1);\n    buff[sizeOfBuff-1] = '\\0';\n} else if (buff != nullptr) {\n    buff[0] = '\\0'; // Set buffer to empty string if source is null\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures the buffer is always in a valid state, even if the source string is missing.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
