            {
              "id": "0f050bab-d8f8-3792-9fa3-e3c6dba22c10",
              "name": "Banned C function detected (strncpy)",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "This finding is considered a genuine vulnerability because the classification indicates a risk of undefined behavior due to the possibility that either the source or destination pointer may be null. The code copies a string from a method call returning a C-style string into a destination buffer using a length parameter, but there are no explicit checks to ensure that either pointer is valid before the operation. If either pointer is null, this can lead to a crash or other unpredictable behavior. The risk is increased because the source is a C-style string, and both the source and destination pointers may be null, as there are no guards in place. The use of a length parameter does not mitigate the risk of null pointer dereference. Therefore, this is a high-confidence, actionable issue.\n\n## In Context Remediation 1\nBefore copying the string, add explicit checks to ensure that both the destination and source pointers are not null. This prevents undefined behavior due to null pointer dereference.\n\n```cpp\nif (name != nullptr && socket != nullptr && socket->get_socket_id().c_str() != nullptr) {\n    strncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), len);\n} else {\n    // Handle error: one or more pointers are null\n}\n```\n\nThis approach ensures that the copy operation is only performed when all pointers are valid, preventing crashes or other undefined behavior.\n\n## In Context Remediation 2\nIf you want to provide a default value or handle the error gracefully when a pointer is null, you can do so as follows:\n\n```cpp\nif (name == nullptr) {\n    // Handle null destination pointer (e.g., log error, return)\n    return;\n}\nif (socket == nullptr || socket->get_socket_id().c_str() == nullptr) {\n    strncpy(name, \"\", len); // Copy empty string or handle as needed\n} else {\n    strncpy(name, socket->get_socket_id().c_str(), len);\n}\n```\n\nThis ensures that the destination buffer is always initialized, even if the source is unavailable.\n\n## References\n- [OWASP C-Based Toolchain Hardening Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n- [CWE-476: NULL Pointer Dereference](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/476.html)\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n___\n"
